      "text": "Coatesville grew around the Brandywine Iron Works, founded by Scottish immigrant William Fleming in the 18th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coatesville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Rebecca Lukens became the first American female CEO of an industrial company when she ran Lukens Steel after her husband’s death.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coatesville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The historic district along South First Avenue houses National Historic Landmark properties, including the 1889‑built Graystone Mansion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.coatesville.org/history-of-the-city-of-coatesville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Every summer, Coatesville hosts the Coatesville Invitational Vintage Grand Prix, a three‑day event showcasing classic cars and motorcycles from the 1900s to the 1980s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
